1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,784
Lights blinking, words printing, touch being checked, all at once. But this chip has

2
00:00:06,784 --> 00:00:08,580
only one processor. How?

3
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:11,720
It's a magic trick, and you're about to learn it.

4
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:18,018
Three children share one toy. A timer rings, and the next child gets it. If the

5
00:00:18,018 --> 00:00:21,840
timer rings fast enough, all three seem to play together.

6
00:00:22,340 --> 00:00:30,576
Our children are tasks. The teacher with the timer is the scheduler. Turns go one,

7
00:00:30,576 --> 00:00:36,100
two, three, one, two, three. That's called round robin.

8
00:00:36,600 --> 00:00:41,729
The project's name says 'cooperative', but notice: here the timer takes the toy

9
00:00:41,729 --> 00:00:44,520
away, whether the child is finished or not.

10
00:00:45,520 --> 00:00:51,575
A timer inside the chip, called SysTick, rings every millisecond: a thousand times a

11
00:00:51,575 --> 00:00:52,080
second.

12
00:00:52,580 --> 00:00:57,052
The chip runs forty-eight million beats a second, so one millisecond is forty-eight

13
00:00:57,052 --> 00:00:57,860
thousand beats.

14
00:00:58,360 --> 00:01:03,880
Each ring does two things: adds one to a tick counter, and asks for a switch.

15
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:10,316
The switch is done by a special helper called PendSV. It's given the lowest

16
00:01:10,316 --> 00:01:13,360
importance, so urgent work finishes first.

17
00:01:13,860 --> 00:01:15,300
Then it swaps the tasks.

18
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:22,332
If someone takes your book, you need a bookmark. Each task has its own private

19
00:01:22,332 --> 00:01:25,580
notebook called a stack, 1,024 bytes here.

20
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:29,520
Before switching, the chip writes down everything it was holding.

21
00:01:30,020 --> 00:01:32,420
The chip's tiny pockets are called registers.

22
00:01:32,920 --> 00:01:37,667
When the interruption arrives, the hardware saves eight of them by itself, including

23
00:01:37,667 --> 00:01:40,040
the program counter: the page you were on.

24
00:01:40,540 --> 00:01:44,540
Our code saves the other eight, R four to R eleven.

25
00:01:45,540 --> 00:01:50,927
Each task has a little card called a task control block. Its first line is the stack

26
00:01:50,927 --> 00:01:53,300
pointer: where the notebook was left.

27
00:01:53,800 --> 00:01:58,040
The switching code expects it to be first, so it mustn't move.

28
00:01:59,040 --> 00:02:06,646
One. Save the outgoing task's remaining registers. Two. Write its notebook position

29
00:02:06,646 --> 00:02:14,160
on its card. Three. Pick the next card. Four. Restore that task's registers. Five.

30
00:02:14,660 --> 00:02:18,740
Return, and the chip continues exactly where that task stopped.

31
00:02:19,740 --> 00:02:24,600
A brand new task has never been interrupted, so we forge a bookmark: a fake saved

32
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:26,940
page pointing to the start of the task.

33
00:02:27,440 --> 00:02:29,200
The switcher can't tell the difference.

34
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:36,357
Trap one: an empty task slot has no bookmark. Switch to it and the chip crashes. So

35
00:02:36,357 --> 00:02:38,360
all three slots get filled.

36
00:02:38,860 --> 00:02:44,491
Trap two: two tasks may talk on the same message line at once, so words can get

37
00:02:44,491 --> 00:02:46,700
mixed. Nothing protects it yet.

38
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:50,160
Quick quiz. After task three, who is next?

39
00:02:50,660 --> 00:02:55,660
Your turn. Pause the video.

40
00:02:55,660 --> 00:02:56,460
Task one.

41
00:02:57,460 --> 00:03:01,140
Timer, bookmark, switch, repeat. Now let's watch your own board do it.

